1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,086
Economic indicators like rates,

2
00:00:02,086 --> 00:00:05,140
oil and jobs are the stock
market's weather forecast.

3
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:07,523
Mr. Park read this forecast backwards,

4
00:00:07,523 --> 00:00:10,685
and in 4 years his 100 million
won shrank to 31 million won.

5
00:00:11,065 --> 00:00:15,434
Over the same 4 years, the
S&P 500 rose more than 40%.

6
00:00:15,814 --> 00:00:18,493
What Mr. Park missed
wasn't the date of a crash,

7
00:00:18,493 --> 00:00:20,670
but which phase the market had entered.

8
00:00:21,050 --> 00:00:23,405
Now look at the five
indicators Mr. Park faced,

9
00:00:23,405 --> 00:00:25,860
and pick which phase the
market was in each time.

10
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:29,023
Keep count of how many
phases you get right.

11
00:00:29,403 --> 00:00:33,492
Now imagine you're Mr. Park, and let's go
back to the day you first bought stocks.

12
00:00:34,492 --> 00:00:37,684
You started investing with
100 million won you had saved.

13
00:00:38,064 --> 00:00:40,589
You put 80 million won
of it into one growth

14
00:00:40,589 --> 00:00:42,999
stock and 20 million won into the S&P 500.

15
00:00:43,379 --> 00:00:45,686
With online shopping booming after COVID,

16
00:00:45,686 --> 00:00:48,611
that growth stock had risen
more than 3x in 2 years.

17
00:00:48,991 --> 00:00:52,218
The S&P 500 had also
just hit a record high.

18
00:00:52,598 --> 00:00:56,928
But the news said the Fed planned
to raise interest rates this year.

19
00:00:57,308 --> 00:00:59,912
Treasuries are IOUs a
government issues to borrow,

20
00:00:59,912 --> 00:01:02,308
and their interest rate
is the Treasury yield.

21
00:01:02,688 --> 00:01:06,678
The 10-year Treasury yield was
already more than 3 times its low.

22
00:01:07,058 --> 00:01:10,416
People around you say, "Stocks
are at a record high. Why worry?"

23
00:01:10,796 --> 00:01:14,788
Do you keep your growth stock as it
is, since it's still a rising phase?

24
00:01:15,168 --> 00:01:19,148
Or do you cut back on your growth stock,
since it's a phase of rising rates?

25
00:01:19,528 --> 00:01:20,468
Make your choice.

26
00:01:20,848 --> 00:01:23,028
3, 2, 1.

27
00:01:23,408 --> 00:01:26,968
In this phase, cutting back on the
growth stock is the right choice.

28
00:01:27,348 --> 00:01:30,723
The date was January 3, 2022,
and the growth stock was Shopify,

29
00:01:30,723 --> 00:01:32,544
the online-store software company.

30
00:01:32,924 --> 00:01:37,023
If you had held on like Mr. Park,
it would have fallen as much as 81%.

31
00:01:37,403 --> 00:01:40,642
Getting back to break-even would
also have taken 3 years and 7 months.

32
00:01:41,022 --> 00:01:43,629
Say you had kept only 20%
of your account in the

33
00:01:43,629 --> 00:01:46,182
growth stock and moved
the rest into the index.

34
00:01:46,562 --> 00:01:50,551
The money moved into the index
would have dropped 24% at worst,

35
00:01:50,551 --> 00:01:52,577
then recovered in under 2 years.

36
00:01:52,957 --> 00:01:58,187
Of course, since 1994, rising rates
haven't always sent stocks sharply lower.

37
00:01:58,567 --> 00:02:00,780
In the five times rate hikes began,

38
00:02:00,780 --> 00:02:04,067
the index fell at most 18%
within a year and a half.

39
00:02:04,447 --> 00:02:05,969
But over the same periods,

40
00:02:05,969 --> 00:02:09,597
the tech-heavy Nasdaq 100 fell
deeper three times out of four.

41
00:02:09,977 --> 00:02:12,990
The more a growth stock is
priced on future earnings,

42
00:02:12,990 --> 00:02:15,094
the harder it swings when rates rise.

43
00:02:15,474 --> 00:02:17,673
So the right choice is to cut back on

44
00:02:17,673 --> 00:02:20,406
expensive growth stocks
first when rates rise.

45
00:02:21,406 --> 00:02:25,906
Two months later, oil prices soared to
their highest level in over 13 years.

46
00:02:26,286 --> 00:02:29,964
The price of a barrel of oil had
jumped more than 60% in two months.

47
00:02:30,344 --> 00:02:34,402
Oil prices signal both consumer prices
and where the economy is heading.

48
00:02:34,782 --> 00:02:40,737
Meanwhile, the S&P 500 had fallen
13%, and your growth stock 62%.

49
00:02:41,117 --> 00:02:43,810
More and more people say
there'll never be another

50
00:02:43,810 --> 00:02:46,449
chance to buy this cheap,
and even borrow to buy.

51
00:02:46,829 --> 00:02:48,803
Do you build up cash with no debt,

52
00:02:48,803 --> 00:02:52,054
since this is a phase where
oil is pushing up inflation?

53
00:02:52,434 --> 00:02:54,371
Or, since it has already fallen a lot,

54
00:02:54,371 --> 00:02:57,174
do you take out a loan to
buy more of the growth stock?

55
00:02:57,554 --> 00:02:58,494
Make your choice.

56
00:02:58,874 --> 00:03:01,054
3, 2, 1.

57
00:03:01,434 --> 00:03:04,824
In this phase, building up cash
with no debt is the right choice.

58
00:03:05,204 --> 00:03:11,099
The date was March 8, 2022, the
day WTI crude topped $123 a barrel.

59
00:03:11,479 --> 00:03:13,741
If you had borrowed to
buy more like Mr. Park,

60
00:03:13,741 --> 00:03:16,594
that money would have been cut
in half seven months later.

61
00:03:16,974 --> 00:03:20,474
The 3-month T-bill yield,
which moves with loan rates,

62
00:03:20,474 --> 00:03:23,196
also rose from 0.4% to 4% in eight months.

63
00:03:23,576 --> 00:03:27,638
Of course, stocks did bounce back
11% over the following month.

64
00:03:28,018 --> 00:03:32,378
But when oil pushes up inflation,
rates tend to rise faster.

65
00:03:32,758 --> 00:03:36,628
Borrowing then leaves you with
rising interest and losses at once.

66
00:03:37,008 --> 00:03:42,048
So the right choice is to expect rates to
rise when oil soars, and to build up cash.

67
00:03:43,048 --> 00:03:46,726
Eight months later, this time the
signal came from the bond market.

68
00:03:47,106 --> 00:03:51,985
The yield on 10-year Treasuries fell
below the yield on three-month bills.

69
00:03:52,365 --> 00:03:55,015
Normally, the longer you
lend, the more you earn.

70
00:03:55,395 --> 00:03:57,592
This is called a yield-curve inversion,

71
00:03:57,592 --> 00:04:00,015
and it's widely seen
as a recession signal.

72
00:04:00,395 --> 00:04:03,915
The S&P 500 is 18% below its record high.

73
00:04:04,295 --> 00:04:06,413
The news is all about a coming recession,

74
00:04:06,413 --> 00:04:09,255
and more people around you
say they've sold everything.

75
00:04:09,635 --> 00:04:13,579
Do you decide the inversion
makes a recession certain,

76
00:04:13,579 --> 00:04:15,405
sell everything and wait?

77
00:04:15,785 --> 00:04:18,492
Or do you take it only as
a warning of higher risk,

78
00:04:18,492 --> 00:04:20,402
and hold to your planned allocation?

79
00:04:20,782 --> 00:04:21,722
Make your choice.

80
00:04:22,102 --> 00:04:24,282
3, 2, 1.

81
00:04:24,662 --> 00:04:27,330
In this phase, taking it
only as a warning and holding

82
00:04:27,330 --> 00:04:29,652
to your planned allocation
is the right choice.

83
00:04:30,032 --> 00:04:35,042
The date was November 10, 2022,
the day the yield curve inverted.

84
00:04:35,422 --> 00:04:38,195
If you had sold everything
and waited like Mr. Park,

85
00:04:38,195 --> 00:04:40,968
you'd have gained just 10%
in interest over 2 years.

86
00:04:41,348 --> 00:04:44,928
Holding the index, you might
have dropped another 4%,

87
00:04:44,928 --> 00:04:47,698
but you'd have been up 56% 2 years later.

88
00:04:48,078 --> 00:04:51,418
Of course, a big bear market
has often followed this signal.

89
00:04:51,798 --> 00:04:56,451
Since 1990, three of the four inversions
were followed by a bear market.

90
00:04:56,831 --> 00:05:01,581
But the decline started anywhere
from one month to 14 months later.

91
00:05:01,961 --> 00:05:05,357
And after the 2022 inversion,
no bear market came.

92
00:05:05,737 --> 00:05:08,318
An inversion doesn't give
you the date of a drop;

93
00:05:08,318 --> 00:05:10,425
it's just a warning that risk has grown.

94
00:05:10,805 --> 00:05:13,811
So the right choice is to read
an inversion as a warning,

95
00:05:13,811 --> 00:05:16,131
not a sell signal, and
keep your allocation.

96
00:05:17,131 --> 00:05:21,539
About eight months later, oil prices
were down 36% from their peak.

97
00:05:21,919 --> 00:05:25,102
The 3-month T-bill yield, which
had soared over the past year,

98
00:05:25,102 --> 00:05:27,259
had barely risen in the last three months.

99
00:05:27,639 --> 00:05:32,749
But the yield curve was still inverted,
so the recession signal was still on.

100
00:05:33,129 --> 00:05:38,248
Stocks are up 28% from the bottom,
but still 5% below their record high.

101
00:05:38,628 --> 00:05:41,985
Your account still has cash
you haven't put into stocks.

102
00:05:42,365 --> 00:05:46,695
Do you wait in cash until the
recession signal goes off?

103
00:05:47,075 --> 00:05:49,458
Or, since rates and oil have calmed down,

104
00:05:49,458 --> 00:05:52,307
do you buy back in up to
your planned allocation?

105
00:05:52,687 --> 00:05:53,627
Make your choice.

106
00:05:54,007 --> 00:05:56,187
3, 2, 1.

107
00:05:56,567 --> 00:06:00,847
In this phase, buying back in up to your
planned allocation is the right choice.

108
00:06:01,227 --> 00:06:06,891
The date was July 26, 2023, the day of
the last hike in that rate-hiking cycle.

109
00:06:07,271 --> 00:06:10,187
If you had waited in cash
for a year like Mr. Park,

110
00:06:10,187 --> 00:06:12,360
you'd have gained just 5% in interest.

111
00:06:12,740 --> 00:06:16,654
The money you put back in would have
dropped nearly 10% three months later,

112
00:06:16,654 --> 00:06:18,220
then been up 20% after a year.

113
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:22,540
The yield-curve inversion went on
for more than a year after that.

114
00:06:22,920 --> 00:06:26,394
Of course, buying while the recession
signal is still on feels uneasy.

115
00:06:26,774 --> 00:06:30,718
But since 1995, in four of the
five times rate hikes stopped,

116
00:06:30,718 --> 00:06:32,787
stocks were higher a year later.

117
00:06:33,167 --> 00:06:35,519
Rather than waiting for
the inversion to end,

118
00:06:35,519 --> 00:06:37,976
what matters is seeing
rates and oil calm down.

119
00:06:38,356 --> 00:06:41,180
So the right choice is
to return to your planned

120
00:06:41,180 --> 00:06:44,181
allocation once rate hikes
stop and oil calms down.

121
00:06:45,181 --> 00:06:49,190
A year later, most of your
money is in the S&P 500.

122
00:06:49,570 --> 00:06:53,407
Then a report says the U.S.
unemployment rate has risen to 4.3%.

123
00:06:53,787 --> 00:06:58,361
With fewer jobs, spending falls too, and
news floods in that a recession has begun.

124
00:06:58,741 --> 00:07:01,448
Stocks fell more than 8% in 3 weeks.

125
00:07:01,828 --> 00:07:06,072
Do you look at several indicators
together and hold as planned?

126
00:07:06,452 --> 00:07:10,221
Or, since a recession has
begun, do you sell everything?

127
00:07:10,601 --> 00:07:11,541
Make your choice.

128
00:07:11,921 --> 00:07:14,101
3, 2, 1.

129
00:07:14,481 --> 00:07:16,749
In this phase, looking
at several indicators

130
00:07:16,749 --> 00:07:19,429
together and holding as
planned is the right choice.

131
00:07:19,809 --> 00:07:21,684
The date was August 5, 2024,

132
00:07:21,684 --> 00:07:25,233
the day stocks plunged after
the unemployment report.

133
00:07:25,613 --> 00:07:31,103
Looking at other indicators that
day, oil was 41% below its 2022 peak.

134
00:07:31,483 --> 00:07:37,035
The 10-year Treasury yield was also
falling, from 4.2% to 3.8% in 3 weeks.

135
00:07:37,415 --> 00:07:39,575
If you had sold everything like Mr. Park,

136
00:07:39,575 --> 00:07:42,893
you'd have gained just 6% in
interest over 1 year and 5 months.

137
00:07:43,273 --> 00:07:46,461
Holding, you'd have regained
the record high in 45

138
00:07:46,461 --> 00:07:49,458
days and been up 34%
after 1 year and 5 months.

139
00:07:49,838 --> 00:07:52,668
Of course, this could have been
the real start of a recession.

140
00:07:53,048 --> 00:07:56,390
But since 1990, stocks have
fallen more than 8% within

141
00:07:56,390 --> 00:07:59,299
a month from near a record
high eighteen times.

142
00:07:59,679 --> 00:08:03,794
Fourteen of those did not turn into
a bear market of more than 20%.

143
00:08:04,174 --> 00:08:07,504
So the right choice is not to
panic-sell over unemployment alone,

144
00:08:07,504 --> 00:08:09,195
but to look at oil and rates too.

145
00:08:10,195 --> 00:08:13,274
How many phases did you
get right in this test?

146
00:08:13,654 --> 00:08:16,495
If you got all five, you
read phases from indicators

147
00:08:16,495 --> 00:08:18,953
and adjust your allocation
to risk: you pass.

148
00:08:19,333 --> 00:08:20,772
If you got three or four,

149
00:08:20,772 --> 00:08:24,513
you tend to ignore signals or
overreact to a single one: caution.

150
00:08:24,893 --> 00:08:27,189
If you got two or fewer, like Mr. Park,

151
00:08:27,189 --> 00:08:30,780
you're likely to miss warning
signs and lose money: you fail.

152
00:08:31,780 --> 00:08:34,386
As this test shows,
don't lock the barn door

153
00:08:34,386 --> 00:08:37,111
after the horse has
bolted: reading the phase,

154
00:08:37,111 --> 00:08:39,835
not the date, from
indicators and cutting risk

155
00:08:39,835 --> 00:08:42,560
in advance is the way
to get rich with stocks.

156
00:08:42,940 --> 00:08:45,150
I wish you all successful investing.

157
00:08:45,530 --> 00:08:47,060
Thank you for watching.
